                      "summary": "Round 3 (fixed re-run of the rejected CUDA-graph direction): replaced run(inputs) with a bounded, fully eager in-place chain on a single fresh buffer. Baseline issues mul/mul/add/sub/relu plus a redundant .clone() (6 kernels); the candidate issues mul -> add_(alpha=0.75) -> sub_(0.125) -> relu_() (4 kernels) and allocates exactly one output tensor. No graph capture, no auxiliary streams, no cached/aliased output buffers, so the shared-GPU runner cannot be destabilized the way candidate-02/03 were, and repeated calls with differing shapes cannot alias. Inputs are never written in place, so reference() on the same dict stays valid.",
                      "diagnosis": {
                        "summary": "Baseline latency 14.368us (naive_v0) is dominated by per-op launch/Python dispatch overhead, not by memory traffic: 32x256 FP32 is 32KB per tensor, so all six kernels are launch-bound. The baseline wastes one full kernel on .clone() and materializes two extra temporaries (x*1.25 and y*0.75) that force separate multiplies. Collapsing to one buffer with an in-place chain removes 2 of 6 launches and 2 intermediate allocations. candidate-01 measured 11.264us with a similar allocation-only change, which confirms the per-launch cost is roughly 1.5us/op; the remaining wins must come from op-count reduction, which is what this diff does. The previous round's failures (candidate-02 CUDA-graph replay, candidate-03 follow-up) were runner terminations during/around graph capture on the shared GPU, an infrastructure-fragile direction abandoned here.",
                        "bottlenecks": [
                          "Per-kernel launch + Python dispatch overhead on a 32KB memory-bound elementwise op (~1.5us per launch observed)",
                          "Redundant .clone() launch in the baseline",
                          "Two extra full-size temporaries for x*1.25 and y*0.75 instead of one reusable buffer"
                        ]
                      },
